
# The ordinary entry point: `npm run generate`.
#
# Everything this project declares about itself lives in sdk-base.aontu. This
# file exists to add exactly one value — the seneca-provider target is OFF —
# so that a plain `generate` is portable to any checkout.
#
# WHY IT MUST BE OFF BY DEFAULT. `main: kit: target: 'seneca-provider':
# output: path` (see sdk-base.aontu) points at a SIBLING repo,
# `../../seneca/solardemo-provider`, relative to this repo's root. sdkgen does
# NOT refuse a destination that does not exist — `checkExternalFolders`
# explicitly `continue`s on a missing folder, so generation CREATES it. On a
# checkout that is not `~/Projects/voxgig-sdk/<repo>` beside
# `~/Projects/seneca/`, that traversal lands somewhere arbitrary and a
# provider repo is silently written there. Observed: a clone at
# /home/user/<repo> produced a 48-file /home/seneca/solardemo-provider,
# outside the SDK project entirely.
#
# So the failure mode is NOT the EACCES the August review recorded — that was
# one workspace's luck in landing on a root-owned path. A writable parent gets
# silent creation instead, which is worse: nothing fails.
#
# `active: false` is the documented lever for this — sdkgen calls it "the
# project's only lever to stop the generator writing into a repo it does not
# own". An inactive external target is skipped with an explicit
# `generate-external-skip` log line, its destination is never validated, and
# it is still withheld from the in-tree pass — so it is not relocated into
# `<sdk-repo>/seneca-provider/` either. Generated ts/ and go/ output is
# unchanged by this file.
#
# TO GENERATE THE PROVIDER, on a machine that has the sibling checkout:
#
#   cd .sdk && npm run generate-provider
#
# which reads model/provider.aon — the same base, `active: true` — instead
# of this file. Do not add `active: true` here: it re-breaks every other
# clone, including CI.
@"sdk-base.aon"

main: kit: target: 'seneca-provider': active: false
